{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>See, I have taught you, etc.</b> In tractate Nedarim (37a) our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: Just as I [learned] for free, so you [must teach] for free. They derived this from what is written: ״כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוַּנִי ה׳ אֱלֹהָי״ — what does this come to teach? For would it enter your mind that Moses would command of his own accord? Rather he said: Just as HaShem my Elohim commanded me for free, so I have taught you for free; and from me you shall see and thus you shall do — to teach for free. For the concept of mutual responsibility, that all Israel are guarantors for one another, requires this matter, for if one single individual sins, the many bear his iniquity. And this matter is alluded to in what is written: ״רְאֵה״ — as if speaking to an individual, and afterward he said ״לִמַּדְתִּי אֶתְכֶם״ to the many — rather, to say that if one individual sins, he is judged among us [i.e., borne by all of us]. And in this manner it will be explained, with the help of HaShem, below at the verse ״רְאֵה אָנֹכִי נֹתֵן לִפְנֵיכֶם״ (Deuteronomy 11:26)." "And he said, <b>See, I have taught you statutes and judgments, etc.</b> The statutes are the commandments whose reason is not revealed, and the judgments are from what is grasped by reason. And concerning them he said the word ״כִּי״ twice, as though giving a reason for each one independently. Concerning the statutes, which he mentioned first, he said: ״כִּי הִיא חָכְמַתְכֶם וּבִינַתְכֶם לְעֵינֵי הָעַמִּים אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁמְעוּן אֵת כָּל הַחֻקִּים הָאֵלֶּה וְאָמְרוּ רַק עַם חָכָם וְנָבוֹן הַגּוֹי הַגָּדוֹל הַזֶּה״ — the explanation of the matter being: if it should arise in your hearts to say, since the nations mock Israel over the observance of the statutes, if so there is no substance to them — for sometimes a person refrains from a certain thing because of mockers — concerning this he said: On the contrary, the nations will praise you for observing the statutes. For there are statutes that are hidden from the eye of every living creature, such as the red heifer, of which it is written (Numbers 19:2): ״וְיִקְחוּ אֵלֶיךָ פָרָה״ — a statute for all of them; even King Solomon said of it: ״אָמַרְתִּי אֶחְכָּמָה וְהִיא רְחוֹקָה מִמֶּנִּי״ (Ecclesiastes 7:23). But to Moses the secret of the cow was revealed — hear from this that the rest of the statutes are revealed to the elite of the generation in whom HaShem has placed wisdom. And what Rashi explained in parashat Chukkat (19:2), that the Satan and the nations mock over it — it is true that this is so for one who hears this statute alone, and the nations ask: What is this statute to you? And you have no answer in your mouth to reply to them; obviously they will mock and deride. But the peoples who hear all these statutes, and most of them have a reason — surely they will not deride, for most of them will give testimony concerning some of them, saying: Just as these statutes have a hidden pleasantness, so too the red cow and all like it have a reason; and even if it is not revealed to the majority of the common people, nevertheless the secret of HaShem is revealed to those who fear Him." "<b>And they will say: Only a wise and understanding people, etc.</b> This is well: all the other nations, who are occupied with matters of wealth and honor and governance and wars — it is no wonder if they sometimes go astray regarding divinity, for the majority of their pursuits are not in wisdoms. But Israel are devoid of all those lesser perfections, and wisdom alone is their garment to their skin and lies in their bosom, and all their occupation is only in wisdoms. How is it possible that elders who acquired wisdom like them would engage in those idle things? Rather, surely they know those secrets, and there is none among us who knows the extent. And this is the force of ״רַק״ — to exclude other matters of perfection, to say that they have no perfection except that they are a wise and understanding people. Or, the word ״רַק״ comes to exclude all the nations, and it turns back upon the word ״הַזֶּה״ — to say: only this great nation is a wise and understanding people, and not we together with it, for there is none among us who knows the extent. And they would attribute the deficiency to the limited grasp of their comprehension." "And concerning the judgments which he mentioned, he said: ״כִּי מִי גוֹי גָּדוֹל אֲשֶׁר לוֹ אֱלֹהִים קְרוֹבִים״ etc. — for if the nations should say: What advantage do you have in the judgments laid down in the Torah? Does not every nation have its customs and legal system? — concerning this he said: You shall know that our judgments possess divine perfection, as it is written (Psalms 82:1): ״אֱלֹהִים נִצָּב בַּעֲדַת אֵל״, and from here they learned that the Holy One, blessed be He, stands in the assembly of judges. For if the judge calls out to HaShem regarding any powerful oppressor, a man of arm who storms against the judge, HaShem answers him from His holy abode — for HaShem pleads their cause, and the judgment belongs to Elohim. Concerning this he said: ״אֲשֶׁר לוֹ אֱלֹהִים קְרוֹבִים אֵלָיו כַּה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ בְּכָל קָרְאֵנוּ אֵלָיו״ — something not found in the judgments of the nations." "<b>And what great nation is there that has statutes and judgments so righteous.</b> He is saying that the judgments of HaShem are true; they are righteous together with the statutes — for in these two parts they do not oppose one another, but both as one are righteous. And furthermore, the statutes are a wonder-testimony concerning the judgments: just as there is a hidden secret in the statutes, so too there is a concealed secret in the judgments, for those who fear HaShem and think upon His name."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:55.519311139Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}